-- title:
--   Lemma 3.1 — Nishimori identity $\mathbb E[\langle f(\theta,\sigma^1,\dots,\sigma^a)\rangle\mid\mathcal F]=\langle f(\sigma^{a+1},\sigma^1,\dots,\sigma^a)\rangle$, and $R_{1\theta}\overset d=R_{12}$
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal F$ be the σ-field generated by the observations being retained. Conditional on $\mathcal F$, let $\sigma^1,\dots,\sigma^{a+1}$ be independent samples from $\mathcal L(\theta\mid\mathcal F)$, independent also of $\theta$ conditionally on $\mathcal F$. For every bounded function $f$ of $a+1$ configurations, which may also depend measurably on $\mathcal F$,
--
--   $$\mathbb E\big[\langle f(\theta,\sigma^1,\dots,\sigma^a)\rangle\,\big|\,\mathcal F\big]=\langle f(\sigma^{a+1},\sigma^1,\dots,\sigma^a)\rangle .$$
--
--   In particular, $R_{1\theta}$ and $R_{12}$ have the same distribution after averaging over the observations. The identity remains valid when the retained observations include revealed coordinates of $\theta$.
--
--   The identity lets the planted signal be replaced by a posterior replica; it is used throughout the overlap and cavity estimates.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The first conjunct is stated for any finite signal space with a prior, observed through any Markov kernel (the retained observations, revealed coordinates included), in integrated form: the integrals of both sides over every event $\{o\in B\}$ of $\mathcal F$ agree (equivalent to the conditional identity, the right side being $\mathcal F$-measurable). The second conjunct is the distributional identity in the Gaussian model of §3.1, for every $N$ and $r,t\ge0$: the planted averages of $\langle\mathbf 1\{R_{1\theta}\in U\}\rangle$ and $\langle\mathbf 1\{R_{12}\in U\}\rangle$ agree for every Borel set $U$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the observations being retained (the Gaussian model of §3.1, or it together with revealed coordinates) → any finite signal space Θ with prior π observed through any Markov kernel κ into any measurable space; reason: specializes to every setting the paper uses, including revealed coordinates
--   - encoding: E[⟨f(θ, σ¹..σᵃ)⟩ | ℱ] = ⟨f(σ^{a+1}, σ¹..σᵃ)⟩ → equality of the integrals over {o ∈ B} for every measurable B; reason: equivalent: the right side is ℱ-measurable and determined by its integrals over ℱ-events
--   - encoding: R_{1θ} and R_{12} have the same distribution after averaging over the observations → for every Borel U, E⟨1{R_{1θ} ∈ U}⟩ = E⟨1{R_{12} ∈ U}⟩ in the Gaussian model; reason: definition of equality in distribution
-- source:
--   Z. Li, Efficient Posterior Sampling for ℤ₂ Synchronization, arXiv:2610.08199 (held version, sha256 536ad4d8…), Lemma 3.1, p.14; Gaussian model §3.1, p.14

